FILE: doc/modal-displayer.md
FEATURE: modal-example-expansion
DATE: 2026-08-04
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'modal-scroll-lock'

================================================================================
CHANGE 1 — Expand the blur overlay section
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
The component adds the `vxKit` class (a CSS `blur` filter) to `pageContainer` while open and removes it on close. `pageContainer` must wrap your page content but **not** the modal content, or the modal blurs too.

NEW_SECTION
Instead of tinting the overlay, this blurs the page behind it. The component adds the `vxKit` class (a CSS `blur` filter) to `pageContainer` while a modal is open and removes it on close, and it leaves the overlay's own background unset so the blur is what you see.

Two requirements:

- **`pageContainer` must wrap your page content but not the modal content**, or the dialog blurs along with the page. Keep your modal markup outside it — the overlay itself is appended to `<body>`, so it is never affected.
- **`config.overlayStyle` is ignored** in blur mode, since the overlay is deliberately left transparent.

With stacking, the blur is held for as long as any open layer wants it and is lifted only when no remaining layer targets that container — so opening and closing a nested modal never flickers the blur off and on.

Configuration is snapshotted when a modal opens, so switching `overlayBackgroundType` takes effect from the next open onwards rather than on the modal already displayed.

REASON
Blur is now exercised by the example, so the guide has to answer the questions a
reader will have once they try it. Three facts were missing and are not deducible
from the API surface, since every config property is write-only:

- overlayStyle is silently ignored in blur mode. The module only applies a background
  to the overlay when overlayBackgroundType is "color", so a consumer who sets both
  would see their overlayStyle have no effect and have no way to discover why.
- The blur is reference-checked against the whole stack rather than removed by the
  closing layer, so a nested open and close does not flicker it. This is behaviour
  introduced by the stacking work and was only recorded in that changelog.
- Config is snapshotted per layer, so switching the mode does not affect a modal
  already on screen. Without this, the control in the example reads as broken.

The note that the overlay itself is appended to <body> is added because it is the
reason the "must not wrap modal content" rule is satisfiable at all - a reader might
otherwise assume the overlay is inside their container and conclude the two
requirements conflict.

================================================================================
CHANGE 2 — Update the example pointer
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
See [`examples/modal/`](../examples/modal/) — all four effects, stacked modals, per-trigger widths, a tall scrolling modal and open/close callbacks. Serve the repository root over HTTP and open `/examples/modal/index.html`:

NEW_SECTION
See [`examples/modal/`](../examples/modal/) — seven modals covering all four effects, a three-deep stack, both overlay background modes, two instances sharing one stack, per-trigger widths, a tall scrolling modal and open/close callbacks. Serve the repository root over HTTP and open `/examples/modal/index.html`:

REASON
The example gained four modals, a second instance and the overlay mode control. The
pointer is the guide's only description of what the demo contains, so it is updated
to match; naming the three-deep stack and the shared stack in particular tells a
reader that the two hardest behaviours to reason about are demonstrable rather than
theoretical.
